    "narrative": "For tonight\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** is the cornerstone of our lineup with an elite score of 85.94. Facing Feltner, whose xERA of 4.96 suggests a prime opportunity for damage, **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a high-upside matchup that plays perfectly into our need for stolen base potential and run production. **Mickey Moniak** serves as our secondary engine at 75.98, buoyed significantly by a robust +14% platoon advantage against Kelly and a favorable park factor, making him a high-efficiency start that anchors our outfield. **Gabriel Moreno** rounds out the top tier at 71.17, as his spot in the #3 hole and the matchup against a struggling Feltner make him an ideal candidate to capitalize on RBI opportunities.\n\nThe marginal start decision of the day centered on **Austin Riley**, who scored 58.02. He was the most difficult exclusion from the \"Strong\" start tier, ultimately hindered by his low position in the order and the tactical disadvantage of facing Thornton, which, despite the pitcher\u2019s mediocre xERA, creates a negative platoon effect that dampened his raw output. We are also monitoring the lineup status of **Zach McKinstry** (48.10) and **Seiya Suzuki** (64.08); while **Zach McKinstry** remains a risky play due to a bottom-order projection, his speed profile offers a floor-preserving utility for our SB goals, provided the projected #9 spot holds. Conversely, we are navigating a depleted bench as **James Wood**, **Trevor Story**, and **Giancarlo Stanton** remain sidelined on the IL, placing additional pressure on our active roster to maintain our pace in the standings without the benefit of those high-ceiling substitutes.\n\nWe are operating with a cautious eye on **Max Muncy** (43.71) and **Cole Young** (47.70) tonight, as both are tasked with facing Rasmussen. Despite Rasmussen\u2019s 3.17 xERA being one of the tougher tests on the board, we are starting them to maintain our game cap efficiency; however, their scores sit right on the razor's edge of the \"Below floor\" designation. We remain invested in the long-term development of prospects like **Dalton Rushing**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte**, alongside cornerstones like **Vinnie Pasquantino**, all of whom are being managed with an eye toward future production as we balance today\u2019s category needs with our league-wide constraints.",
